29-Sep-2026

Dear Dr. Lizardo:

We have received the reviews on your manuscript, =E2=80=9CPatriotism as a C=
hoice Process: National Pride After 9/11=E2=80=9D (MS SPQ-26-0082). After c=
areful consideration of your work as well as the reviewers=E2=80=99 comment=
s, we have decided not to accept your manuscript for publication in Social =
Psychology Quarterly (SPQ). However, we would like to encourage you to subm=
it a revised manuscript that takes into account the issues raised below and=
 in the reviews.

Both reviewers see much of value in the paper. However, both feel that the =
work is undertheorized; that is, the mechanisms underlying the theoretical =
processes are not sufficiently spelled out. In addition, both reviewers thi=
nk that the paper needs to be a little more polished in its presentation an=
d that the flow of the paper could be improved. Additionally, one reviewer =
believes, and we agree, that more detail and justification is needed in the=
 methods section.

In addition to the issues we outline above, please attend carefully to othe=
r comments provided by the reviewers. When you submit your revised manuscri=
pt please indicate how you have addressed these comments in in a separate, =
revision memo. We are grateful for the feedback of our dedicated reviewers,=
 and we hope that you find their comments helpful. This revision memo shoul=
d also provide a clear justification for any reviewer comments that you do =
not address in your revisions.

After you have had a chance to consider the reviewers=E2=80=99 comments, pl=
ease let us know when we should expect your revised manuscript. Keep in min=
d, in our experience shorter turnaround times on a revision ensure that the=
 original reviewers are still available to give advice on your revision and=
 delays of more than two months can cause difficulties. If you anticipate n=
eeding a longer period to complete your revisions, please notify us. If you=
 decide not to resubmit your manuscript, please let us know that as well.

Once again, thank you for submitting your manuscript to Social Psychology Q=
uarterly, and we look forward to receiving your revised paper.

Kind Regards,

Lisa Slattery Walker and Joseph Dippong
Editors in Chief
Social Psychology Quarterly
socpsychq@charlotte.edu


Reviewer(s)' Comments to Author:

Reviewer: 1

Comments to the Author
Review of =E2=80=98Patriotism as a Choice Process: National Pride after 9/1=
1=E2=80=99

Thank you for the opportunity to review the manuscript entitled =E2=80=98Pa=
triotism as a Choice Process: National Pride after 9/11=E2=80=99. The paper=
 aims to investigates how feelings of national pride and nationalism develo=
ped among different subgroups of US-Americans in the time period surroundin=
g 9/11. It contrasts two theoretical expectations: Following the =E2=80=98i=
rrational patriotism=E2=80=99 and =E2=80=98needs-based attachment=E2=80=99 =
perspectives, it is expected that lower-educated and socially less integrat=
ed individuals should display larger increases in national pride after 9/11=
. By contrast, following the =E2=80=98choice-process=E2=80=99 approach, the=
 reverse is expected. The authors test these hypotheses using two rounds of=
 GSS data collected in 1996 and 2004. Based on regression models, they find=
 support for the choice-process approach.=20

There are several things I like about this paper: First, it asks an interes=
ting research question. Second, it draws upon interesting theories to deriv=
e the main arguments. Third, it is clearly written.=20

However, there are also several major issues with the present version of th=
e manuscript. I refer to the most important ones below.=20

First, the theoretical arguments lack clear substance. While the overarchin=
g theories are briefly referred to, the underlying mechanisms by which 9/11=
 should spark more/less nationalism and pride among different subgroups rem=
ain unclear. Moreover, too little attention is paid to social-psychological=
 differences between the two outcome variables. What is the benefit of stud=
ying both of them? How do they differ? Furthermore, in several cases, the e=
xpectations are not backed up by appropriate arguments. For example, in the=
 first paragraph on p. 5, it is unclear, why exactly we should expect =E2=
=80=9Ca weakening of the proximal rule and the emergence of a distal rule.=
=E2=80=9D. In the following paragraph, it is not specified, why the sense o=
f saliency =E2=80=9Cshould be stronger for individuals embedded in nested s=
ubgroups.=E2=80=9D

Second, there is virtually no discussion of the 9/11 event itself. Why is i=
t important? How should it shape feelings of national pride? What was the c=
ontext? At the moment, 9/11 appears somewhat out of the blue in the manuscr=
ipt. More broadly, I miss a discussion of how the study fits in with other =
studies on 9/11 and other major events as well as their effects on feelings=
 of pride and nationalism.=20

Third, the empirical analysis is too simplistic and not transparent enough.=
 The authors run logistic regression models on two waves of GSS data. There=
 are several problems:=20
-=09Many things happened between 1996 and 2004. 9/11 was certainly an impor=
tant event. But there were other important events, too (e.g., war under the=
 Bush administration), which might explain changes in pride in armed forces=
. It is by no means obvious that all the change observed can be attributed =
to 9/11.=20
-=09Why are logistic regressions used? As I understand the data and variabl=
es section, the outcome variables are continuous scales. Why do you not use=
 linear regressions instead?
-=09The methods section that explains the modelling approach is completely =
missing.=20
-=09There are no descriptive tables/plots, which renders it difficult to fu=
lly make sense of the regression results. The authors included a placeholde=
r, but the actual table is not there.=20
-=09Modelling choices are not justified. E.g., why are the factors underlyi=
ng one outcome scale dichotomized, but not those underlying the other?

Minor points:
The introduction stops halfway through. Its last paragraph simply reads =E2=
=80=9CIn this paper,=E2=80=9D.

The authors talk about =E2=80=98excessive=E2=80=99 attachment to the nation=
. What are we talking about here? What would be =E2=80=98healthy=E2=80=99 a=
ttachment=E2=80=99? What is the reference point?


Reviewer: 2

Comments to the Author
This research note examines the extent that a national crisis generates mor=
e patriotic national feelings among people from different social status bac=
kgrounds.  Theoretically, the paper contrasts (1) a =E2=80=9Cneeds-based=E2=
=80=9D approach, traced to the authoritarian personality (Adorna) and suppl=
emented by the positive self-evaluation or self enhancement motivation in s=
ocial identity theory (Hogg, Terry and others), with (2) the =E2=80=9Cchoic=
e process approach of Lawler (1992) that emphasizes the role subgroup ties =
within the larger group (nation in this case).  The former emphasizes the e=
motional needs (e.g., self-enhancement) met or served by patriotic national=
 sentiments, and the =E2=80=9Cchoice process=E2=80=9D approach (Lawler) emp=
hasizes the role of autonomy and sense of control in nested group structure=
s, where ties to local (=E2=80=9Cproximal=E2=80=9D) groups can spread to mo=
re removed (=E2=80=9Cdistal=E2=80=9D groups) such as the nation. The author=
 hypothesizes that from a needs-based approach a national threat or crisis =
will increase patriotic feelings more among the lower status individuals th=
an higher status individuals; whereas from the choice process approach, pat=
riotic feelings will increase more among those of higher status because of =
positive ties to nested subgroups. =20

The paper makes an interesting and insightful comparison between these two =
approaches. To test the hypothesis, the author uses General Social Surveys =
from 1996 and 2004 that includes questions on national sentiments.  The aut=
hor has to rely on =E2=80=9Cproxies=E2=80=9D to measure key variables of re=
levance to the hypothesis.  Education level is the proxy for status, and me=
mberships in voluntary associations is the measure of subgroup ties.  One c=
an certainly quibble about how good are these proxies but they strike me as=
 reasonable approximations.  Similarly, the logic underlying the prediction=
s for these proxies and the connection to the two theoretical approaches se=
ems quite reasonable, if not perfect. Finally, the results offer a fairly c=
lear and consistent picture though, of course, they must be considered sugg=
estive rather than definitive --yet sufficient for a research note. =20

However, the paper needs some improvement.  The theorizing of both approach=
es has some gaps or omissions that call for a bit of elaboration or more ex=
plicitness along several lines =E2=80=93 as follows:=20
1.=09The needs-based approach is tied primarily to the authoritarian person=
ality tradition in the paper, and then a footnote on social identity theory=
 and self-categorization theory claims it also is a needs-based theory beca=
use of its core self-enhancement motivation.  The author is not explicit ab=
out the connection between the authoritarian personality tradition and soci=
al identity theory.  The footnote also muddies the waters a bit by referenc=
es to the sense of efficacy.  This raises a question about how distinct are=
 the two theoretical approaches because the importance of a =E2=80=9Csense =
of control=E2=80=9D is a premise of the choice process theory.  A solution,=
 in my view, is to explicitly emphasize the positive self-esteem/self-enhan=
cement motivation that is foundational in social identity theory. This seem=
s to be justified by the theory, and it also most clearly fits the =E2=80=
=9Cneeds based=E2=80=9D aspect of authoritarian personality.  Finally, the =
general content of the footnote should be integrated into the text. =20

2.=09In the treatment of choice process theory, the paper needs to introduc=
e the distinction between ties to proximal and distal groups earlier in the=
 paper.  The choice process theory predicts stronger ties to proximal group=
s and treats ties to larger groups as problematic.  How and when these ties=
 spread upward to larger groups is not completely resolved in the 1992 pape=
r.   This paper suggests that a serious threat to the larger distal group i=
s one answer to this question and finds evidence to support this claim.  Ho=
wever, this subgroup focus of the theory needs to be explicated more clearl=
y early in the paper, rather than emerge later as is currently the case.  I=
n this context, the results for subgroup embeddedness (membership in volunt=
ary associations) are an important contribution of the paper.  Such results=
 offer clear support the idea that subgroup (proximal) ties tend to foster =
stronger distal (larger group) ties when the larger group faces a serious t=
hreat or crisis.  =20

3.=09The paper could use a careful re-write to improve the flow and clarify=
 some of the key points.  The writing is OK but not great.  It is rough in =
places and there are long sentences that can be a bit confusing.   My impre=
ssion =E2=80=93 right or wrong =E2=80=93 is that the author may have rushed=
 the final stages of preparing the manuscript.  Another way to say this is:=
  Try give the paper a bit more polish.=20
=20
4.=09Delete the supplemental models on political ideology.  It doesn=E2=80=
=99t add much and it isn=E2=80=99t really that necessary.  It is too much o=
f a side trip.  If really important to the author, a brief footnote and off=
er to send the results to the reader upon request should be sufficient.
